course: dictionary
lesson: 15065
title: Word focus: revoke
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Revoke means to officially take back or cancel something that was given, allowed or decided. The clues are the words around it. «The council decided to revoke the licence of the café after the food checks failed.» Decided and licence tell you that someone in charge is cancelling a formal permission.
explain: Revoke is a verb, so its form changes with time. Today we revoke, yesterday we revoked, and someone is revoking now. «Last week the teacher revoked my permission to use the computer room.» The permission was taken away, so revoked is the past tense.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping in a simple word. Replace revoke with cancel: «The council decided to cancel the licence» still makes sense, so cancel is a good synonym here. Revoke often means cancelling something that someone had been officially given or allowed.
fact: Revoke means to officially cancel a law, permission, licence or decision.
fact: Revoke is a verb: revoke, revokes, revoked, revoking.
fact: «The hotel revoked our booking» means the hotel officially cancelled it.
fact: In card games, to revoke means to break a rule by not following suit when you could.
fact: Cancel is a close synonym, but revoke is more formal and is often used for licences, permissions and laws.
exercise: «The government agreed to revoke the old ban on bikes in the park.» What does revoke mean here? | To ask for a picnic | *To officially cancel the ban | To make the ban stronger | To paint the park gates
exercise: Which word is the past tense of revoke? | revoking | revokes | *revoked | revoke
exercise: Mia's teacher said her computer privilege would be revoked for a week. What does revoked mean? | Given to a friend | Wrapped up in paper | *Taken away officially | Made bigger
test: «The hotel revoked our booking when we arrived late.» What does revoked suggest? | Enjoyed | Painted | *Cancelled | Doubled
test: In a card game, what does it mean to revoke? | To shuffle the pack | To win the trick | To deal the cards | *To break a rule by not following suit when you could
exam: Which sentence uses revoke correctly? | We revoked a big cake for tea. | *The club revoked his membership after he broke the rules. | The sun revoked at night. | The cat revoked the milk from the bowl.
